// A NUL byte inside a str is DATA: str carries its own length and `==`
// walks exactly len bytes, so equality does not stop where C's strcmp
// would. Every pair below differs first at a position strcmp would never
// reach or reports equal at a byte C calls terminator.
//
//   "a\0b" vs "a\0c"   differ INSIDE, past the NUL -- strcmp says EQUAL,
//                      having stopped at position 1
//   "a"     vs "a\0"   same visible prefix, different lengths -- strcmp
//                      also says EQUAL
//
// Both wrong answers are plausible booleans from real implementations:
// lowering `==` to C's strcmp, or memcmp'ing only the shorter length.
// The embedded-NUL row is the one this tree could plausibly get wrong,
// because the emitted C hands literals around as `(unsigned char *)`
// arrays and nothing in the generated code remembers they are not C
// strings.
//
// Breaks it: emit `(strcmp(a.data, b.data) == 0)` instead of the Eq call
// -- row 3 turns true; or memcmp over min(len) -- row 5 turns false.

main = (env: Env) Res<i32, AllocError> {
    nul = "a\0b";
    println("{}", nul.len);
    println("{}", "a" == "a");
    println("{}", "a\0b" == "a\0c");
    println("{}", "a" == "a\0");
    println("{}", nul == "a\0b");
    println("{}", "\0" == "");
    println("{}", "\0".len);
    Ok(0);
}
